\par \pard\plain \s17\loch\sl276\slmult1\sb0\sa140\fi0\li0\lin0\ri0\rin0\sb0\sa0\ltrpar{\hich\af4\loch\cf1\f4\loch
Assuming 100% efficiency, using all of the debris in orbit around Earth, 1.) would it be realistically possible to build an O'Neill Cylinder of any size at all (regardless of how few inhabitants), and if so, 2.) what would be the maximum number of inhabitants it could house?}
\par \pard\plain \s17\loch\sl276\slmult1\sb0\sa140\fi0\li0\lin0\ri0\rin0\sb0\sa0\ltrpar\hich\af4\loch\cf1\f4\loch

\par \pard\plain \s17\loch\sl276\slmult1\sb0\sa140\fi0\li0\lin0\ri0\rin0\sb0\sa0\ltrpar{\hich\af4\loch\cf1\f4\loch
Yes, it would be realistically possible to build a very small O'Neill-style rotating cylinder (or similar habitat) using all the orbital debris mass with 100% efficiency. However, it would be tiny\u8212\'97more like a proof-of-concept or single-family-to-small-village habitat than anything resembling the classic multi-kilometer O'Neill designs\u8212\'97and limited by practical engineering challenges beyond raw mass (e.g., material properties, assembly in orbit, structural integrity under spin, radiation protection if not in a shielded orbit, life support closure, etc.). }
