Orbit choice: In very low Earth orbit (below Van Allen belts), shielding mass drops dramatically, freeing budget for more structure/volume. }
\par \pard\plain \s17\loch\sl276\slmult1\sb0\sa140\fi0\li709\lin709\ri0\rin0\sb0\sa0\ltrpar{\hich\af4\loch\cf1\f4\loch
thespacereview.com}
\par \pard\plain \s17\loch\sl276\slmult1\sb0\sa140{\listtext\pard\plain \rtlch\af6 \ltrch\hich\af6\loch\f6\dbch\af6 \u57352\'3f\tab}\ilvl0\ls11 \fi-283\li0\lin0\tx709\fi-283\li709\lin709\ri0\rin0\sb0\sa0\ltrpar{\hich\af4\loch\cf1\f4\loch
Materials: Debris is a mix of aluminum, titanium, steel, composites\u8212\'97not all ideal, and processing (melting, forming) requires energy/infrastructure already in orbit.}
\par \pard\plain \s17\loch\sl276\slmult1\sb0\sa140{\listtext\pard\plain \rtlch\af6 \ltrch\hich\af6\loch\f6\dbch\af6 \u57352\'3f\tab}\ilvl0\ls11 \fi-283\li0\lin0\tx709\fi-283\li709\lin709\ri0\rin0\sb0\sa0\ltrpar{\hich\af4\loch\cf1\f4\loch
Engineering: Spin stresses, pressure vessel integrity, thermal/radiation management, docking, mirrors for sunlight, etc. A 100% efficient mass use ignores these overheads.}
\par \pard\plain \s17\loch\sl276\slmult1\sb0\sa140{\listtext\pard\plain \rtlch\af6 \ltrch\hich\af6\loch\f6\dbch\af6 \u57352\'3f\tab}\ilvl0\ls11 \fi-283\li0\lin0\tx709\fi-283\li709\lin709\ri0\rin0\sb0\sa0\ltrpar{\hich\af4\loch\cf1\f4\loch
Life support: Closed-loop systems for air/water/food are feasible at small scale but need testing at this size.}
\par \pard\plain \s17\loch\sl276\slmult1\sb0\sa140{\listtext\pard\plain \rtlch\af6 \ltrch\hich\af6\loch\f6\dbch\af6 \u57352\'3f\tab}\ilvl0\ls11 \fi-283\li0\lin0\tx709\fi-283\li709\lin709\ri0\rin0\sb0\sa0\ltrpar{\hich\af4\loch\cf1\f4\loch
This would be an extreme "use every scrap" project\u8212\'97far beyond current capabilities, but in principle, the mass exists for a minimal rotating habitat.}
\par \pard\plain \s17\loch\sl276\slmult1\sb0\sa140\fi0\li0\lin0\ri0\rin0\sb0\sa0\ltrpar{\hich\af4\loch\cf1\f4\loch
In short: Yes for a small one (proof-of-concept scale), supporting perhaps hundreds comfortably or low thousands cramped/optimized. It would be a remarkable achievement but a far cry from self-sustaining cities in space. Scaling up would require lunar/asteroid resources. Designs like Kalpana One or spacecalcs simulators provide good starting points for further exploration.}
