\par \pard\plain \s17\loch\sl276\slmult1\sb0\sa140\fi0\li0\lin0\ri0\rin0\sb0\sa0\ltrpar\hich\af4\loch\cf1\f4\loch

\par \pard\plain \s17\loch\sl276\slmult1\sb0\sa140\fi0\li0\lin0\ri0\rin0\sb0\sa0\ltrpar{\hich\af4\loch\cf1\f4\loch
Assuming that within the interior of a rotating ring on an O'Neill Cylinder, given a human with a height of 6 foot 5 inches, while standing, would experience a near-imperceptible difference in gravity at the person's head vs. at the person's feet, would it be possible to build an O'Neill Cylinder to comfortably house up to 200 humans plus 200 robots? If so, would it be possible to house more than that, and if so, up to how many, while still maintaining 10 meters squared for each inhabitant}
\par \pard\plain \s17\loch\sl276\slmult1\sb0\sa140\fi0\li0\lin0\ri0\rin0\sb0\sa0\ltrpar{\hich\af4\loch\cf1\f4\loch
Thoughts}
\par \pard\plain \s17\loch\sl276\slmult1\sb0\sa140\fi0\li0\lin0\ri0\rin0\sb0\sa0\ltrpar\hich\af4\loch\cf1\f4\loch

\par \pard\plain \s17\loch\sl276\slmult1\sb0\sa140\fi0\li0\lin0\ri0\rin0\sb0\sa0\ltrpar{\hich\af4\loch\cf1\f4\loch
Yes, it would be realistically possible to build a (relatively) small O'Neill-style rotating cylinder (or similar habitat like a scaled Kalpana One) capable of comfortably housing 200 humans + 200 robots while providing near-1G artificial gravity with a near-imperceptible head-to-feet difference for a 6'5" (\u8776\'3f1.96 m) person. It could also scale to house significantly more people under the 10 m\u178\'b2 per inhabitant constraint.Gravity Uniformity (Head vs. Feet)The gravity gradient in a rotating habitat is approximately \u916\'3fg/g \u8776\'3f h / r, where h is height (1.96 m) and r is the cylinder radius to the floor. }
\par \pard\plain \s17\loch\sl276\slmult1\sb0\sa140\fi0\li0\lin0\ri0\rin0\ltrpar{\hich\af4\loch\cf1\f4\loch
worldbuilding.stackexchange.com +1}
\par \pard\plain \s17\loch\sl276\slmult1\sb0\sa140{\listtext\pard\plain \rtlch\af6 \ltrch\hich\af6\loch\f6\dbch\af6 \u57352\'3f\tab}\ilvl0\ls12 \fi-283\li0\lin0\tx709\fi-283\li709\lin709\ri0\rin0\sb0\sa0\ltrpar{\hich\af4\loch\cf1\f4\loch
For a ~2% difference (widely considered acceptable/near-imperceptible for long-term habitation): Minimum r \u8776\'3f 100 m.}
\par \pard\plain \s17\loch\sl276\slmult1\sb0\sa140{\listtext\pard\plain \rtlch\af6 \ltrch\hich\af6\loch\f6\dbch\af6 \u57352\'3f\tab}\ilvl0\ls12 \fi-283\li0\lin0\tx709\fi-283\li709\lin709\ri0\rin0\sb0\sa0\ltrpar{\hich\af4\loch\cf1\f4\loch
For a 1% difference (even more comfortable): r \u8776\'3f 200 m.}
\par \pard\plain \s17\loch\sl276\slmult1\sb0\sa140{\listtext\pard\plain \rtlch\af6 \ltrch\hich\af6\loch\f6\dbch\af6 \u57352\'3f\tab}\ilvl0\ls12 \fi-283\li0\lin0\tx709\fi-283\li709\lin709\ri0\rin0\sb0\sa0\ltrpar{\hich\af4\loch\cf1\f4\loch
For <0.5% (truly imperceptible): r \u8776\'3f 400 m+.}
\par \pard\plain \s17\loch\sl276\slmult1\sb0\sa140\fi0\li0\lin0\ri0\rin0\sb0\sa0\ltrpar\hich\af4\loch\cf1\f4\loch

\par \pard\plain \s17\loch\sl276\slmult1\sb0\sa140\fi0\li0\lin0\ri0\rin0\sb0\sa0\ltrpar{\hich\af4\loch\cf1\f4\loch
At r = 100\u8211\'96250 m and ~1G (9.8 m/s\u178\'b2), rotation rates fall in the 2\u8211\'963 rpm range. This keeps Coriolis effects low enough for comfort (minimal nausea, head-turn issues, or balance problems for most people), especially with some acclimation. Many studies and designs (e.g., Kalpana One at 250 m radius, 2 rpm) target this regime for permanent living. }
\par \pard\plain \s17\loch\sl276\slmult1\sb0\sa140\fi0\li0\lin0\ri0\rin0\sb0\sa0\ltrpar{\hich\af4\loch\cf1\f4\loch
nss.org +2}
