\par \pard\plain \s17\loch\sl276\slmult1\sb0\sa140\fi0\li0\lin0\ri0\rin0\sb0\sa0\ltrpar{\hich\af4\loch\cf1\f4\loch
Okay, so, within the confines of the mass of the debris in orbit around planet Earth, assuming all gravitational as well as food, water, and sanitation conditions provided, at a radius of 100 m for comfortable simulated gravity at 1 G, what's the largest population that could realistically live comfortably aboard such a resulting Cylinder?}
\par \pard\plain \s17\loch\sl276\slmult1\sb0\sa140\fi0\li0\lin0\ri0\rin0\sb0\sa0\ltrpar\hich\af4\loch\cf1\f4\loch

\par \pard\plain \s17\loch\sl276\slmult1\sb0\sa140\fi0\li0\lin0\ri0\rin0\sb0\sa0\ltrpar{\hich\af4\loch\cf1\f4\loch
With the ~16,200 metric tonnes of orbital debris (100% efficient reuse, optimistic), a 100 m radius cylinder at 1G could realistically support a comfortable population of roughly 200\u8211\'96800 humans plus a similar or larger number of robots. This assumes low Earth orbit (partial magnetosphere protection minimizing radiation shielding), advanced materials from debris (aluminum, composites, etc.), closed-loop life support, hydroponics/vertical farming, and heavy robot automation. }
\par \pard\plain \s17\loch\sl276\slmult1\sb0\sa140\fi0\li0\lin0\ri0\rin0\sb0\sa0\ltrpar{\hich\af4\loch\cf1\f4\loch
